# {{.Name}} — Rollback Runbook

> Generated by forge new ts-service (forge {{.ForgeVer}})
> Keep this document up to date every time you ship a breaking change.

## Pre-rollback checklist

- [ ] Confirm the incident scope (which tenants / features are affected)
- [ ] Notify on-call rotation (`#oncall` Slack channel)
- [ ] Open an incident record: `forge incident open "<title>"`
- [ ] Capture current production metrics snapshot (latency, error rate, DB locks)

## Database rollback

```bash
# See current migration state
forge migrate status --env production

# Roll back one migration
forge migrate down --env production --steps 1

# Roll back to a specific migration
forge migrate down --env production --to 20260101000000
```

> **Warning:** rolling back a migration that removed columns or tables may cause data loss.
> Review the migration's `-- migrate:down` section before running.

## Application rollback

Replace the commands below with your platform's deployment CLI.

```bash
# Example: Railway
railway rollback --service {{.Name}} --to <deployment-id>

# Example: Fly.io
fly releases --app {{.Name}}
fly deploy --app {{.Name}} --image <previous-image>

# Example: Cloud Run
gcloud run services update-traffic {{.Name}} \
  --to-revisions <revision>=100 \
  --region us-central1
```

## Verification after rollback

```bash
# Health gate
forge doctor --env production

# Smoke tests
npm run test:security -- --reporter=verbose

# Check error rate in observability dashboard
# (link your dashboard URL here)
```

## Post-rollback

- [ ] Update incident record with rollback time: `forge incident update <id>`
- [ ] File a postmortem within 48 h: `forge postmortem draft "<title>"`
- [ ] Identify root cause and add regression test before re-deploying

